WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
How well can a clothespin pick up floating cereal?

2
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.880
Give your child a chance to try it.

3
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.420
Then see what happens when you offer a slotted spoon.

4
00:00:12.383 --> 00:00:17.343
In about thirty-five minutes at the table, your child tests five kitchen tools as

5
00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:22.983
bird beaks. They practice guessing first, counting fairly, and comparing results.

6
00:00:24.303 --> 00:00:28.003
They also connect simple kitchen models to real birds outside.

7
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:30.657
Stay with your child throughout.

8
00:00:32.037 --> 00:00:36.097
Keep small pieces and water containers away from children under four.

9
00:00:37.357 --> 00:00:38.957
Small pieces can choke them.

10
00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:41.637
Call nine one one if choking.

11
00:00:42.937 --> 00:00:44.737
Check food allergies before setup.

12
00:00:45.997 --> 00:00:47.337
Skip red kidney beans.

13
00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:50.437
Keep all station items out of mouths.

14
00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:53.737
Set five stations around the table.

15
00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:57.877
Put dry lima or pinto beans in a shallow dish.

16
00:00:59.297 --> 00:01:01.097
Float cereal loops in a bowl of water.

17
00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:05.677
Put pasta fish in a pan with three centimeters of water.

18
00:01:06.897 --> 00:01:08.097
Cut any sponge yourself.

19
00:01:09.357 --> 00:01:11.157
Press yarn halfway into play dough.

20
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.540
Set out a clothespin, slotted spoon, straw, tongs, and tweezers.

21
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.540
Every child needs their own straw.

22
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.000
Fill a narrow plastic bottle with water.

23
00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:25.780
Set an empty cup beside it.

24
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:31.467
Give your child a small cup to hold in one hand and a tool to hold in the other.

25
00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:34.967
Move food to the cup using only the tool.

26
00:01:36.287 --> 00:01:38.227
In this game the cup is the bird’s stomach.

27
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.660
Start with pond food.

28
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.040
Look at the clothespin and the slotted spoon.

29
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.520
Which do you think will pick up more floating cereal in the same time?

30
00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:57.163
At each station, have your child pick three tools.

31
00:01:58.603 --> 00:02:00.803
Ask, “Which beak do you think will get the most food?”

32
00:02:01.343 --> 00:02:02.343
Write down their guess.

33
00:02:03.503 --> 00:02:05.243
Try each tool for thirty seconds.

34
00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:08.663
Count what lands in the cup, then tip it back.

35
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:13.027
Dip the straw in the bottle and cover the top with a finger.

36
00:02:14.247 --> 00:02:16.847
Lift it over the empty cup and uncover it.

37
00:02:18.047 --> 00:02:19.047
Count each strawful.

38
00:02:20.307 --> 00:02:22.247
The straw models a hummingbird’s reach.

39
00:02:23.527 --> 00:02:26.067
A hummingbird uses its tongue to collect nectar.

40
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.960
Check the pond counts first.

41
00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.820
Circle the best tool for each food.

42
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.360
Different beak shapes work well for different foods.

43
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.860
A cardinal cracks seeds.

44
00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.800
A mallard strains food from water.

45
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.240
A heron grabs fish.

46
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.060
A hummingbird reaches nectar.

47
00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.960
A wren picks insects off bark.

48
00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:52.933
If your child asks about changing beaks, try this.

49
00:02:54.193 --> 00:02:56.653
“Can you grow longer fingers to reach a high shelf?”

50
00:02:57.373 --> 00:03:00.233
A bird’s bill can grow and wear down during its life.

51
00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:05.353
A change in food cannot make one bird grow a different kind of bill.

52
00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:10.153
Over many generations, bill shapes can change across a group.

53
00:03:10.153 --> 00:03:11.153
How can you grow longer fingers to reach a high shelf?

54
00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:15.287
Keep straws out of mouths and tweezer tips down and away from faces.

55
00:03:16.827 --> 00:03:18.527
Skip toothpicks and other sharp points.

56
00:03:19.807 --> 00:03:22.627
Wipe up spills and pick up dropped pieces right away.

57
00:03:23.967 --> 00:03:25.947
Empty the bowls and pan when you finish.

58
00:03:27.287 --> 00:03:28.827
Wash hands with soap and water.

59
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.300
Ask, “What helped one tool work better?”

60
00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.660
Your child can use the counts to explain what they noticed.

61
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.400
Watch how the tools move, too.

62
00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.620
A heron waits for a fish to come close.
